## Database Type Selection (SQL vs NoSQL)

### 1. Online Banking System → SQL (Relational Database)
**Justification:**
- Requires ACID properties (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability)
- Handles secure transactions (e.g., money transfer)
- Ensures strong data integrity and consistency
- Data is highly structured (accounts, users, transactions)

**Examples:** MySQL, PostgreSQL  

---

### 2. Social Media Platform → NoSQL
**Justification:**
- Handles large volumes of unstructured data (posts, images, comments)
- Needs high scalability for millions of users
- Flexible schema for frequent feature updates
- Fast read/write performance

**Examples:** MongoDB, Cassandra  

---

### 3. Real-Time Chat Application → NoSQL
**Justification:**
- Supports real-time messaging
- High write throughput (many messages per second)
- Semi-structured data (messages, timestamps, users)
- Easy horizontal scaling

**Examples:** Firebase, Redis, MongoDB  

---

### 4. Hospital Patient Records → SQL
**Justification:**
- Requires high accuracy and consistency (critical medical data)
- Structured data (patients, doctors, prescriptions)
- Strong relationships between entities
- Ensures data security and compliance

**Examples:** PostgreSQL, Oracle  

---

### 5. IoT Sensor Data Collection → NoSQL
**Justification:**
- Handles massive continuous data streams
- Time-series and semi-structured data
- Requires high scalability and fast ingestion
- Flexible schema for different devices

**Examples:** InfluxDB, Cassandra  

---

## Summary Table

| Use Case                     | Database Type | Key Reason                     |
|----------------------------|--------------|--------------------------------|
| Online Banking System      | SQL          | Transactions & consistency     |
| Social Media Platform      | NoSQL        | Scalability & flexibility      |
| Real-Time Chat Application | NoSQL        | Real-time & high speed         |
| Hospital Patient Records   | SQL          | Accuracy & relationships       |
| IoT Sensor Data           | NoSQL        | High volume & scalability      |
